/* A compound assignment whose target has no unboxed value. `Number n = 1;
 * n += 1` would need the target's value before it can add to it, and a
 * reference type that is not one of the eight wrappers has no unboxing
 * conversion (JLS 5.1.8), so there is nothing to add: javac 21 rejects all
 * three of these with "bad operand types for binary operator '+='", and the
 * second type it prints is the operand's, an int.
 *
 * Teyru used to accept them: the checker recorded an error type and said
 * nothing, and the back end then combined two references. The C back end
 * emitted `(*p) += 1` on a `C_teyru_Number **`, which is valid C -- pointer
 * arithmetic that walks the box by one element and corrupts the reference --
 * so the program compiled and then answered nonsense or died. Refusing is what
 * the rule of the language and javac both say, and it is the same check that
 * TY-TYP-0064 already makes for the binary operator. */
class F {
  int k = 1
}

class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Number n = 1
    n += 1
    Object o = 1
    o -= 1
    F f = new F()
    f += 1
    System.out.println(n)
    System.out.println(o)
    System.out.println(f)
  }
}
